The rule:

```text
App → Features → Shared/Domain
Features → Repositories → Core/Networking
Views → ViewModels → Repositories → APIClient
DesignSystem can be used everywhere
Core should not know about Features
```

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    App[App / RootView] --> Shell[Shell / Navigation]
    Shell --> Features[Feature Views]
    Features --> ViewModels[Feature ViewModels]
    ViewModels --> Repositories[Repositories]
    Repositories --> APIClient[Core Networking]
    APIClient --> Backend[Spring Boot API]

    Repositories --> Mappers[Mappers]
    Mappers --> Domain[Domain Models]
    Mappers --> DTOs[API DTOs]

    Features --> DesignSystem[Design System]
    App --> Session[Session Store]
    Session --> AuthRepo[Auth Repository]
    AuthRepo --> TokenStore[Keychain Token Store]
    AuthRepo --> APIClient
```

## What I would fix first

### 1. Split `ScubaAPI.swift`

This is your highest-value refactor.

Right now `ScubaAPI` does too much:

```text
networking
auth refresh
endpoint methods
response decoding
domain conversion
media URL resolution
```

Better split:

```text
APIClient
  does raw HTTP request/response

AuthRepository
  login/register/logout/refresh

FeedRepository
  feed/createPost/toggleLike/comments

ProfileRepository
  currentProfile/profile/follow

MessagingRepository
  conversations/messages/sendMessage

DiscoveryRepository
  profiles/locations/shops/learn

Mappers
  DTO → Domain
```

Bad long-term shape:

```swift
session.api.feed()
session.api.toggleLike(postID: post.id)
session.api.currentProfile()
```

Better shape:

```swift
feedRepository.loadFeed()
feedRepository.toggleLike(postID: post.id)
profileRepository.currentProfile()
```

Then your views no longer care that the backend is REST, generated OpenAPI, mocked data, or later GraphQL/WebSocket.

---

### 2. Stop putting DTOs and domain models in the same file

`ScubaModels.swift` currently mixes backend DTOs and app domain models.

Separate them because they have different jobs.

```text
DTOs = exact backend shape
Domain = clean app shape
Mapper = conversion boundary
```

Example:

```text
FeedPostViewDTO
  postId: Int?
  caption: String?
  mediaUrls: [String]?

Post
  id: Post.ID
  content: String
  mediaUrls: [URL]
  createdAt: Date
```

This matters because your backend can change without forcing your whole UI to change.

---

### 3. Do not default missing backend IDs to `0`

This is important.

You currently have patterns like:

```swift
id: input.postId ?? 0
```

That is dangerous because multiple failed/malformed records can become the same identity.

Better:

```swift
guard let id = input.postId else {
    throw MappingError.missingRequiredField("postId")
}
```

Same issue with fallback IDs using `hashValue`. Swift’s `hashValue` is not stable across launches. That can break caching, diffing, navigation, and SwiftUI identity.

For production: **invalid server data should fail loudly at the boundary**, not silently become fake data.

---

### 4. Fix date parsing behavior

Current behavior:

```swift
return Date()
```

when parsing fails.

That hides backend bugs. A bad timestamp becomes “right now,” which is misleading.

Better:

```swift
static func fromAPI(_ value: String?) throws -> Date
```

or:

```swift
static func fromAPI(_ value: String?) -> Date?
```

Then your mapper decides whether that field is required.

---

### 5. Move API calls out of SwiftUI views

Your views are currently doing too much async state work.

Example pattern today:

```swift
@State private var posts: [Post] = []
@State private var isLoading = true
@State private var errorMessage: String?

.task { await loadFeed(...) }
```

Production shape:

```swift
@State private var viewModel: FeedViewModel
```

Then:

```swift
@MainActor
final class FeedViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published private(set) var state: Loadable<[Post]> = .idle

    private let repository: FeedRepository

    func load() async { ... }
    func like(_ post: Post) async { ... }
}
```

Views should mostly render state. ViewModels coordinate state. Repositories talk to backend.

---

### 6. Rename `AppSession` to `SessionStore`

`AppSession` is currently decent, but it is becoming a god object.

Keep it focused on:

```text
auth state
current user
session restoration
logout
global badge counts
toast coordination, maybe
```

Do not let it become:

```text
all app state
all repositories
all navigation
all feature state
```

Better:

```text
SessionStore
BadgeStore
ToastStore
```

But do not split too early. For now:

```text
SessionStore = auth + current user
AppUIStore = toast/global UI
BadgeStore = unread counts
```

---

### 7. Treat `DesignSystem` as its own module

`ScubaTheme.swift` is over 500 lines and contains theme colors, modifiers, buttons, remote images, avatars, empty states, toast views, etc.

That is good work, but production structure should be:

```text
DesignSystem/
  Theme/
    ScubaTheme.swift
    Typography.swift
    Spacing.swift

  Components/
    ScubaButton.swift
    FloatingTextField.swift
    AvatarView.swift
    RemoteImage.swift
    EmptyStateView.swift
    ToastView.swift

  Modifiers/
    GlassPanel.swift
    AppBackground.swift
```

This makes your UI system reusable without polluting app logic.

---

### 8. Use proper environment config

Current base URL fallback:

```swift
http://127.0.0.1:8080
```

That works in simulator sometimes, but not reliably for real-device testing unless the backend is actually reachable from the phone.

Use:

```text
Config/
  Debug.xcconfig
  Staging.xcconfig
  Release.xcconfig
```

Example:

```text
SCUBAPAL_API_BASE_URL = http://192.168.x.x:8080
```

Then production gets:

```text
SCUBAPAL_API_BASE_URL = https://api.scubapal.com
```

Do not hard-code production URLs in Swift files.

---

### 9. Clean Git/project hygiene

Your zip contains things that should not be committed or shared:

```text
.DS_Store
__MACOSX/
xcuserdata/
UserInterfaceState.xcuserstate
```

Add this to `.gitignore`:

```gitignore
.DS_Store
__MACOSX/
DerivedData/
*.xcuserstate
xcuserdata/
```

Also, your deployment target appears set to `26.5`. Unless you intentionally only want the newest OS, lower it to a realistic minimum once you know your SwiftUI/API requirements.

---

## Production-style workflow

Use this order:

```text
1. Make it run
2. Move API boundary out of views
3. Split DTO/domain/mappers
4. Add repositories
5. Add ViewModels
6. Add tests around mappers and auth refresh
7. Add CI
8. Add generated OpenAPI client
9. Add real staging/prod configs
```

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Prototype works] --> B[Split ScubaAPI]
    B --> C[DTO / Domain / Mapper boundary]
    C --> D[Repositories]
    D --> E[Feature ViewModels]
    E --> F[Tests]
    F --> G[CI]
    G --> H[OpenAPI generated client]
    H --> I[Staging / Production workflow]
```

## Best near-term target

Your next goal should be:

**45% progress target:** split `ScubaAPI.swift` and `ScubaModels.swift`.

Do this first:

```text
Core/Networking/
  APIClient.swift
  APIError.swift
  HTTPMethod.swift

Core/Auth/
  TokenStore.swift
  KeychainTokenStore.swift

Shared/DTO/
  AuthDTOs.swift
  FeedDTOs.swift
  ProfileDTOs.swift

Shared/Domain/
  User.swift
  Post.swift
  Comment.swift

Shared/Mappers/
  UserMapper.swift
  PostMapper.swift

Features/Feed/
  FeedRepository.swift
  FeedViewModel.swift
```

Do **not** over-engineer with Swift packages yet. Use folders first. Once the app crosses roughly 10k–15k lines or multiple people/modules, then promote pieces into local Swift packages like:

```text
ScubaCore
ScubaDesignSystem
ScubaAPI
ScubaFeatures
```

## The simple rule

Your final architecture should feel like this:

```text
Views do not fetch.
ViewModels do not decode JSON.
Repositories do not render UI.
APIClient does not know app features.
DTOs do not leak into views.
Domain models do not mirror backend accidents.
Core stays small.
Features own feature behavior.
DesignSystem owns visual language.
```

That is the production direction.
